  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why now</p>
      <h2>LLMs ship duplication faster than you can spot it.</h2>
      <p>A coding agent's strongest instinct is pattern-matching — which means it will happily generate the same function three times, in three files, under three names. Human review does not scale past a few hundred thousand tokens of generated surface area. CodeDedup does.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-grid">
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Worst-first ranking</h3>
        <p>Score = clone size × clone count × spanned LOC. The top row of the report is always where the largest payoff lives. No hunting.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Tree-sitter parsing</h3>
        <p>Regex on source code is illegal here. Every language is parsed with a real grammar, normalized, and fingerprinted at the AST level.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Four clone types</h3>
        <p>Type-1 identical, Type-2 renamed, Type-3 near-miss, Type-4 semantic. Hashing + LSH + embedding fusion catches all four.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Agent-ready output</h3>
        <p>JSON is the canonical format. Every cluster carries byte ranges, signals, scores, and a short natural-language summary an agent can act on.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Incremental by design</h3>
        <p>Cache keyed by <code>(file_hash, model_id, model_version)</code>. Unchanged files are skipped. Ready for the MCP/LSP daemon that lands in v2.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Three output formats</h3>
        <p>Structured JSON, line-oriented plain text, standalone HTML. One schema, three renderers. No ANSI, no paging, no surprises.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The pipeline</p>
        <h2>From source to ranked report in one deterministic pass.</h2>
        <p>No magic, no model-of-the-week. Each stage is cache-keyed, byte-range-addressable, and replayable. Swap the embedding model without re-parsing.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline" aria-label="Pipeline stages">
        <span>discover</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>parse</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>normalize</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>fingerprint</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>cluster</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>LSH</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>embed</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>fuse</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>rank</span><span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span>render</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Evidence, not vibes</p>
      <h2>Every cluster comes with the signals that fired.</h2>
      <p>A CodeDedup cluster is not a string match — it is a decision backed by three independent signals. You read the signal strip, you decide.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="two-up">
<pre><span class="c-dim">// UserRepository.cs — 120–180</span>
public async Task&lt;User&gt; GetById(int id)
{
    var entity = await _ctx.Users
        .Where(u =&gt; u.Id == id)
        .FirstOrDefaultAsync();
    if (entity == null)
        throw new NotFoundException();
    return _mapper.Map&lt;User&gt;(entity);
}</pre>
<pre><span class="c-dim">// ProductRepository.cs — 58–118</span>
public async Task&lt;Product&gt; GetById(int id)
{
    var entity = await _ctx.Products
        .Where(p =&gt; p.Id == id)
        .FirstOrDefaultAsync();
    if (entity == null)
        throw new NotFoundException();
    return _mapper.Map&lt;Product&gt;(entity);
}</pre>
